prompt:
{
  "public_instructions": "You translate natural-language access-control requirements into Cedar policies using the supplied schema. Cedar denies by default. An applicable forbid overrides every permit. Guard optional attributes with has before reading them. Preserve the schema's entity, set, and attribute types. Use only the supplied task information. Do not use tools, files, search, additional model calls, or external feedback. Produce a single final answer as a JSON object with exactly one field, policy, whose value is the complete Cedar policy text. Do not include explanations or Markdown fences.
",
  "harness": {
    "instruction_blocks": [
      "Translate the access-control requirements into a complete Cedar policy set. Identify each action, eligible principal, resource type, required condition, prohibition, and exception. Use only entity types, attributes, actions, and representations supported by the schema. Implement the stated permissions and restrictions together.",
      "Before answering, check the policy mentally for both unauthorized permissions and missing required permissions. Keep distinct action rules separate when their conditions differ. Apply exceptions only to the restriction they qualify. Return one final policy set."
    ],
    "input_order": [
      "requirements",
      "schema"
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Every required action is covered.",
      "Role, ownership, membership, and context conditions follow the requirements.",
      "Prohibitions and exceptions compose correctly.",
      "Optional attribute reads are guarded and Cedar syntax is valid."
    ]
  },
  "requirements": "The system manages modification of cloud resources in an IAM environment.

The people involved are owners, developers, operators, and org admins. One person may hold both the owner and developer role at the same time.

The resources are cloud resources, secrets, and org units. Every cloud resource records its owner, org unit, tags, environment, and temporary grant.

Each requester carries their organization membership.

Each request carries source IP, whether MFA was used, and session age.

Owners, developers, and operators may modify a cloud resource only when MFA was used.

When MFA was not used, owners, developers, and operators must not modify a cloud resource.

Owners, developers, and operators may modify a cloud resource outside production, or in production when it has been promoted.

Owners, developers, and operators must not modify a production cloud resource that has not been promoted unless the requester belongs under that cloud resource's org unit.

When the requester belongs under the cloud resource's org unit, the production promotion requirement does not block the modification.

A request by an owner to modify a cloud resource that does not trip any prohibition above must be able to succeed.",
  "schema": "entity Role enum ["owner", "developer", "operator", "orgAdmin"];

entity Environment enum ["production", "nonProduction"];

entity User {
    roles: Set<Role>,
    orgMembership: OrgUnit,
};

entity OrgUnit in [OrgUnit];

entity CloudResource {
    owner: User,
    orgUnit: OrgUnit,
    tags: Set<String>,
    environment: Environment,
    temporaryGrant: Bool,
    promoted: Bool,
};

entity Secret;

action modifyCloudResource appliesTo {
    principal: [User],
    resource: [CloudResource],
    context: {
        sourceIp: ipaddr,
        mfaUsed: Bool,
        sessionAge: duration,
    },
};
"
}
